Ladybird now finds the baseline of a line box from wherever each
box’s vertical-align puts it, under every keyword. And it counts
the layout bounds of the inline boxes around a fragment where their
own alignment shifts them. Blink does the same: InlineLayoutStateStack::
ApplyBaselineShift() moves the metrics of each box by its baseline
shift, and the line box takes the union of those metrics. In WebKit,
LineBoxVerticalAligner::logicalTopOffsetFromParentBaseline() gives
the top of each box relative to its parent’s baseline for every keyword,
and computeLineBoxLogicalHeight() finds the line box from those.
Gecko’s nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignFrames() places each frame by its
vertical-align, and grows the span’s minBCoord and maxBCoord to hold it.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit 26.5 ran
the cases of the test added here, and agree on all 20 of them; Ladybird
now gives what they give. All of that is from running them; the function
names are from reading the sources.
